# PocketBase 0.40.2 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# READ THIS FIRST: PocketBase is not configured by environment variables.
# Its real configuration surface is (a) CLI flags on `pocketbase serve`
# (--dir, --http, --publicDir, --hooksDir, --encryptionEnv, --queryTimeout,
# --dev) and (b) the superuser dashboard at /_/, where SMTP, S3 file
# storage, backups, OAuth2 providers, rate limits, trusted-proxy handling,
# token lifetimes and log retention all live, stored in the database, not
# in env. This file is short because the honest env surface IS short.
#
# Source: `grep -rn 'os.Getenv(\|os.LookupEnv('` across the pocketbase
# v0.40.2 Go source. It returns exactly five app reads
# (PB_THUMBS_MAX_WORKERS, PB_THUMBS_MAX_WAIT, PB_FILES_DELETE_MAX_WORKERS,
# PB_ID_TOKEN_LEEWAY, and the settings-encryption key looked up by whatever
# name --encryptionEnv was given) plus GOCACHE, which is only used to guess
# whether the binary was started by `go run` and means nothing in a
# container. Cross-checked against the image's entrypoint.sh at the build
# revision 55f3ba94e1eacff1b5cfc84d8780859e14214cc0, which adds four more.
# Nothing else is read. Any PB_* name not listed below is invented.
#
# IMAGE PROVENANCE: PocketBase publishes no official Docker image. The
# compose pins the community image ghcr.io/muchobien/pocketbase:0.40.2
# (digest sha256:d634793070b34bb8..., built 2026-09-02T13:19:30Z, roughly
# a minute after upstream published v0.40.2). It is a 20-line Dockerfile
# that unzips the official pocketbase_<ver>_linux_<arch>.zip asset onto
# alpine:3.24.1 and adds one entrypoint script, rebuilt hourly by a GitHub
# Actions cron that watches the pocketbase release feed. Do not float this
# back to `latest`: that cron picks the newest release by creation time and
# PocketBase
# maintains two active lines (0.40.x current and 0.22.x LTS, often released
# minutes apart), so `latest` can and has moved BACKWARD onto the 0.22 line.
#
# FIRST-RUN OWNERSHIP: read before exposing this publicly. A fresh
# PocketBase has no superuser. On every `serve` where the superusers
# collection is empty, apis/installer.go creates a hidden internal account
# (__pbinstaller@example.com), mints a 30-minute static auth token for it,
# and PRINTS a claim URL of the form `<public-url>/_/#/pbinstall/<token>`
# to stdout, i.e. into the container logs. Whoever opens that URL first
# creates the real superuser and owns the instance. The token is the only
# thing guarding it, and anyone who can read the deploy logs has it. After
# 30 minutes the link is dead until the container restarts. To skip that
# race entirely, set PB_ADMIN_EMAIL and PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD below: the
# entrypoint runs `pocketbase superuser upsert` before `serve`, so the
# account exists before the first request and the installer never arms.
# That is the only superuser bootstrap this image supports; there is no
# other env name for it.
#
# The compose sets no environment at all, so every key here is optional and
# empty-by-default unless a real default is shown. It also deliberately
# passes NO `command:`, because the entrypoint only applies its own
# defaults (`serve --http=$PB_HOST:$PB_PORT --dir=/pb_data
# --publicDir=/pb_public --hooksDir=/pb_hooks`) and only runs the superuser
# bootstrap when it is given no arguments or arguments starting with `-`.
# A `command: [serve, ...]` takes the passthrough branch instead, which
# silently drops --dir=/pb_data (data lands in /usr/local/bin/pb_data,
# outside the volume) and skips PB_ADMIN_EMAIL/PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD.
#
# Settings encryption is a flag, not a variable: `--encryptionEnv NAME`
# tells PocketBase to read the 32-character key from the env var you name.
# Because the name is yours to pick and is dead weight without the flag on
# the service command, this schema declares no key for it.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Going to production", https://pocketbase.io/docs/going-to-production/)
# ---

# ─── First-run superuser (image entrypoint) ──────────────────────────────

# Email of the superuser the entrypoint creates before starting the server.
# Set together with PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD to claim the instance yourself
# instead of racing whoever reads the installer link in the logs. The
# entrypoint runs `superuser upsert`, so this is applied on EVERY container
# start: leaving both set means each restart re-asserts this email and
# password. Read only when the container gets no command arguments (the
# case in this template's compose).
# @type=email
# @docs("muchobien/pocketbase image: environment variables", https://github.com/muchobien/pocketbase-docker)
PB_ADMIN_EMAIL=
# Password for PB_ADMIN_EMAIL. The `_superusers` password field has Min: 8,
# and the entrypoint runs under `set -e`, so a shorter one aborts the
# container start rather than falling back to the installer. Because
# `upsert` re-runs on every start, changing the password in the dashboard
# while leaving a stale value here silently resets it back on the next
# restart. Clear both once the account exists if you want the dashboard to
# be the source of truth.
# @sensitive
# @type=string(minLength=8)
PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ─── Listen address (image entrypoint) ───────────────────────────────────

# Interface the entrypoint tells `serve` to bind (--http=$PB_HOST:$PB_PORT).
# 0.0.0.0 is what the container network needs; 127.0.0.1 makes the service
# unreachable from outside the container. Not read by PocketBase itself,
# purely an entrypoint convenience.
# @docs("muchobien/pocketbase image: environment variables", https://github.com/muchobien/pocketbase-docker)
PB_HOST=0.0.0.0
# Port the entrypoint tells `serve` to listen on. The compose exposes
# container port 8090 with no override, so this is that port's twin:
# changing it here without also changing the exposed port breaks routing.
# @type=port
PB_PORT=8090

# ─── Thumbnail generation ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Maximum concurrent thumbnail generations (apis/file.go). Empty or <= 0
# falls back to the container's CPU count + 2. Lower it on a small box
# where a burst of first-time thumbnail requests can starve the API.
# @type=number
# @docs("Files handling", https://pocketbase.io/docs/files-handling/)
PB_THUMBS_MAX_WORKERS=
# Seconds a request will wait for a free thumbnail worker before giving up.
# @type=number
PB_THUMBS_MAX_WAIT=60

# ─── File deletion ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Maximum concurrent storage deletions when records or collections that own
# files are deleted (core/base.go). Empty or <= 0 falls back to 2000. Worth
# lowering when files live on S3 and the provider rate-limits deletes.
# @type=number
# @docs("Files handling", https://pocketbase.io/docs/files-handling/)
PB_FILES_DELETE_MAX_WORKERS=2000

# ─── OAuth2 / OIDC ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Clock-skew leeway, in seconds, for the timestamp claims of an OIDC
# provider's id_token (tools/auth/oidc.go). Default 300 (5 minutes).
# Raise it only if OIDC logins fail because this host's clock and the
# provider's disagree; the OAuth2 providers themselves are configured in
# the dashboard, not here.
# @type=number
# @docs("Authentication", https://pocketbase.io/docs/authentication/)
PB_ID_TOKEN_LEEWAY=300

# ─── Timezone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Not read by PocketBase's own code: this is the Go runtime's timezone,
# resolved against the tzdata package the image installs. It sets what
# local time means for log timestamps and for the cron expressions behind
# scheduled backups. Empty leaves the container on UTC.
TZ=
